  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low will pass east of the New Jersey \nShore Sunday through Sunday night.\n\nWith onshore flow, there's been sufficient moisture pushing on\nshore in order to allow patchy fog to have broken out and has \nbeen locally dense at times but is expected to break shortly \nafter day break per hires guidance. \n\nBased on WV satellite imagery the surface low continues to \norganize off the Florida coast and will track north later this\nafternoon eventually bringing periods of rainfall and gusty \nwinds along the coastal communities.\n\nThe eastern jog in the models continues with the system\nexpecting to be less impactful than previously forecast. The\nmain impacts from the system will be higher winds along the\ncoast, with generally around a half inch of rain expected along\nthe coast. For portions of PA, some of the hi-res guidance is\nsuggesting the may even end up completely dry. Rain moves into \nDelmarva in the late morning, but timing of the bulk of the \nrain looks to be well into the late afternoon. With the eastern\nshift in guidance, flash flooding is generally not expected \neven with the timing being close to high tide. We can't rule out\nsome minor coastal flooding but thats likely only due to the\ncombination of fresh and salt water.\n\nRain tapers off Sunday night, and conditions will dry out on\nMonday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A low pressure system will affect the weather \nfor the Middle Atlantic area on Thanksgiving day. \n\nA low pressure system is expected to bring impactful \nweather to the region around Thanksgiving. \n\nA strong cold front will move through the region on Monday as \nSunday's coastal low departs to the northeast. High pressure will \nbriefly build in to the region in its wake, with much cooler \nconditions expected Tuesday and Wednesday. Highs look to mostly be \nin the 40s and 50s with overnight lows in the 20s to low 30s. \n\nBeginning on Wednesday, the next potential storm of concern will \nbegin to take shape off the Southeast coast. A strong upper-low will \nshift eastward out of the central US, with strong divergence aloft \nfavoring surface cyclogenesis over the relatively warm waters that \nremain. From there, the low is expected to track northeastward \ntowards the Mid-Atlantic through Thursday. Guidance remains quite \nconsistent on the development and rapid deepening of this low, with \nthe primary discrepancies at this point being the exact low track. \nThere is considerable spread among the EC-AIFS and the GEFS suites, \nranging from primarily an inland track to a couple hundred miles \noffshore. This will obviously have significant implications on \nimpacts. \n\nThere is high confidence that as the high that will be centered near \nour region for the first half of the week retreats northward, so \nwill the cold air. So while it is not entirely out of the question \nthat some wintry precipitation could occur for portions of the \nLehigh Valley, Poconos, and northern NJ near the onset of \nprecipitation, the remainder of the area is almost certainly going \nto be looking at all rain. Aside from high confidence in \nprecipitation type, confidence is low overall which is to be \nexpected at this range. It should be noted though that the bulk of \navailable guidance leaves the door open for other significant \nimpacts such as heavy rainfall, strong winds, and coastal flooding, \nparticularly if the low takes on a track close to the coast.  \n\nDetails regarding specific impacts will come into better focus over \nthe coming days, and especially after this weekend's coastal storm \ndeparts.",
